From ddacf1858ec28a7922fbc9efac7db6aebedc46b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tomas Glozar <tglozar@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 21:30:01 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 530/634] IA64: Add a perf_events driver for the IA-64 PMU

perf_events on ia64 has so far been limited to software events and
tracepoints; the hardware PMU has had no driver since perfmon was removed
in commit ecf5b72d5f66 ("ia64: Remove perfmon"). Add one, covering the
counting monitors (PMC/PMD4 and up). The event address registers, the
branch trace buffer, the opcode matchers and the address range checking
perfmon also drove have no equivalent in a perf counter and stay unused.

The monitors are programmed as privileged (pmc.pm=1), which gates them on
psr.pp rather than psr.up, so a monitored task cannot turn counting off
behind perf's back. Counting at kernel level then comes for free by setting
dcr.pp once per CPU: every interruption turns psr.pp back on, so no hook on
the kernel entry paths is needed. User level is what costs something, as
the value an interrupted context resumes with comes from its saved ipsr, so
the incoming task's ipsr.pp is fixed up in ia64_{save,load}_extra() the way
pfm_syst_wide_update_task() used to do it. That is behind a per-CPU count
of monitors that ask for the user privilege level, so IA64_HAS_EXTRA_STATE()
stays false and context switches are undisturbed while perf is idle.

Individual counters are started and stopped by writing pmc.plm, and
pmc0.fr serves as the pmu_enable/pmu_disable hook. Every counter is armed
with pmc.oi, so an overflow freezes the PMU, records itself in pmc0 and
raises IA64_PERFMON_VECTOR; the handler folds the counters, re-arms them
and clears pmc0. Counting events get a sample period of half the counter
range as well, otherwise the 32 bit counters of the first Itaniums would
wrap unnoticed within seconds.

A raw event config is the value of the counter's PMC, with everything the
kernel drives itself masked out, and the event select and unit mask fields
are described in sysfs, so both

	perf stat -e r1200
	perf stat -e cpu/event=0x12/

select CPU_CYCLES.

This commit only knows the architected PMU, whose description is taken from
PAL_PERF_MON_INFO: the number of counters, their width, and the two event
codes PAL reports. That is enough for cycles and instructions on any IA-64
implementation; the per-model descriptions follow.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Tomas Glozar <tglozar@gmail.com>
